      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite a launch announcement of up to 500 words for a new feature of a B2B helpdesk product: a Slack integration that posts a daily summary of open support tickets to a channel of the customer's choice. The audience is existing customers.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n**New: Daily ticket summaries in Slack**\n\nStarting today, [Product] can post a daily summary of your open support tickets directly to any Slack channel you choose.\n\n**Why we built it**\n\nYou told us the same thing in dozens of conversations: your team lives in Slack, but your queue lives in the helpdesk. That gap means stale tickets go unnoticed until a customer follows up, and managers end up manually assembling status updates for standups and leadership.\n\nThe Slack Daily Summary closes that gap. Once a day, at a time you pick, your queue comes to you — no logins, no dashboards, no reminders to check.\n\n**What's in the summary**\n\nEach post gives your team a clear picture of where things stand:\n\n- **Open ticket count**, broken down by status (new, in progress, waiting on customer)\n- **Tickets breaching SLA** in the next 24 hours, with assignee and time remaining\n- **Unassigned tickets** that need an owner\n- **Oldest open tickets**, so nothing quietly ages out\n- **Yesterday's resolved count**, for a quick sense of momentum\n\nEvery ticket in the summary links straight to [Product], so anyone can jump from Slack to the full conversation in one click.\n\n**Set it up in under two minutes**\n\n1. Go to **Settings → Integrations → Slack** and click **Connect**.\n2. Authorize [Product] in your Slack workspace.\n3. Choose a channel, a delivery time, and a time zone.\n4. Save. Your first summary arrives on the next scheduled run.\n\nYou can create multiple summaries if different teams care about different slices of the queue — for example, a Tier 1 channel filtered to unassigned tickets and a leadership channel filtered to SLA risk only. Filters support views, tags, priority, and assignee group.\n\n**A few details worth knowing**\n\n- **Permissions carry over.** The integration respects your existing role-based access rules. Ticket titles appear in Slack, but the summary never includes message bodies, attachments, or customer contact details.\n- **Admin-only setup.** Only workspace admins in [Product] can connect Slack or create summaries, so channel configuration stays controlled.\n- **Quiet days stay quiet.** If your queue is empty, you'll get a short confirmation rather than a wall of headers — or you can suppress empty summaries entirely.\n- **Weekend scheduling.** Choose which days the summary runs. Many teams turn off Saturday and Sunday.\n\n**Availability and pricing**\n\nThe Slack integration is included at no additional cost on Growth and Enterprise plans, and is available today in all regions. Team plan customers can trial it free for 30 days from the Integrations page.\n\n**What's next**\n\nThis is the first step in a broader Slack roadmap. We're currently testing real-time alerts for SLA breaches and new high-priority tickets, plus the ability to reply to a customer directly from a Slack thread. If you'd like early access to either, reply to this email and we'll add you to the beta list.\n\n**Get started**\n\nHead to **Settings → Integrations → Slack**, or read the [setup guide] for screenshots and filter examples. As always, our support team is one ticket away if you hit a snag — and yes, we'll see it in our own Slack summary tomorrow morning.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n**New: Daily Ticket Digest for Slack**\n\nStarting today, you can have a summary of your open support tickets delivered to Slack every morning — no dashboard required.\n\n**What it is**\n\nThe Daily Ticket Digest posts one message per day to a Slack channel you choose. It gives your team a shared, at-a-glance view of where support stands before the day starts, so nobody has to ask \"what's still open?\" in a standup or dig through their inbox to find out.\n\nEach digest includes:\n\n- Total open tickets, with the change since yesterday\n- Breakdown by priority and status (new, waiting on customer, waiting on us)\n- Tickets approaching or past their SLA, listed individually with a direct link\n- Oldest unresolved ticket and how long it's been open\n- Tickets assigned to nobody\n\nEvery ticket mentioned in the digest links straight back to the helpdesk, so you can act on it in one click.\n\n**Setting it up**\n\nIf you already have Slack connected, this takes about a minute:\n\n1. Go to **Settings → Integrations → Slack**\n2. Click **Add Daily Digest**\n3. Pick a channel, a delivery time, and the views you want included\n4. Save\n\nIf you haven't connected Slack yet, you'll be prompted to authorize the app first. Only admins can create or edit digests; anyone in the channel can read them.\n\n**Making it yours**\n\nYou can run more than one digest. Many teams set up a company-wide summary in a general channel and a more detailed one for the support team. Each digest can be filtered by team, queue, tag, or assignee, and scheduled independently — so your EMEA and US teams can each get their own at the right local time.\n\nPrefer weekdays only? Toggle off weekends. Want it quiet when there's nothing to report? Turn on \"Skip if no open tickets.\"\n\n**A few things to know**\n\n- The digest is read-only. It won't create, close, or reassign tickets from Slack.\n- It respects your existing permissions. If a queue is restricted, only digests configured by users with access to it can include its tickets.\n- Delivery times are in your account's timezone, and you can override this per digest.\n- It's available on all plans at no additional cost.\n\n**Why we built it**\n\nThe most common request we heard this year was some version of \"I just want to know what's open without logging in.\" The dashboard is great when you're actively working tickets; it's less great for the manager checking in before a meeting or the engineer who wants to know if their fix cleared the backlog. Putting the summary where people already are — Slack — felt like the right answer.\n\n**Tell us what you think**\n\nThis is the first version, and we'd like to shape it with you. If there's a metric you want included, a format that would work better, or a Teams equivalent you're waiting on (we hear you), reply to this email or leave feedback from the digest settings page.\n\nThanks for building with us.\n\n— The Product Team\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
